WEBVTT

00:00:00.810 --> 00:00:06.220
There's so much I wanted to talk to you about

00:00:00.810 --> 00:00:06.220
hanashitakatta koto takusan atta no ni

00:00:06.220 --> 00:00:11.560
But my mind went blank when I saw your face

00:00:06.220 --> 00:00:11.560
kao miru to wasurechau

00:00:11.560 --> 00:00:20.170
Every time you peek in to pick me up after school my heart lights up

00:00:11.560 --> 00:00:20.170
houkago mukae ni kitekureru no ga ureshikute

00:00:22.280 --> 00:00:27.420
I wonder why I so hopelessly feel this way about you

00:00:22.280 --> 00:00:27.420
doushite kimi ja nakucha dame na no kana

00:00:32.910 --> 00:00:41.170
Maybe it's like how we can't live without the sun or sea

00:00:32.910 --> 00:00:41.170
taiyou ya mizu ga hitsuyou na no to niteru ki ga suru

00:00:35.750 --> 00:00:41.170
Maybe it's like how we can't live without the sun or sea

00:00:35.750 --> 00:00:41.170
taiyou ya mizu ga hitsuyou na no to niteru ki ga suru

00:00:43.670 --> 00:00:48.560
The days have begun to get shorter now

00:00:43.670 --> 00:00:48.560
hi ga mijikaku nattekita ne

00:00:48.560 --> 00:00:56.690
Why must they cut off my time with you?

00:00:48.560 --> 00:00:56.690
mou chotto issho ni itai no ni

00:00:56.690 --> 00:01:02.300
Tomorrow and the day after, and every day forevermore

00:00:56.690 --> 00:01:02.300
ashita mo asatte mo sono saki mo zutto

00:01:02.300 --> 00:01:07.450
I want to keep walking home together like this

00:01:02.300 --> 00:01:07.450
kouyatte kimi to kaeritai

00:01:07.450 --> 00:01:12.900
No matter what the weather was, no matter how my day went

00:01:07.450 --> 00:01:12.900
donna tenki demo donna kibun demo

00:01:12.900 --> 00:01:20.670
The moment you raise your hand to wave goodbye

00:01:12.900 --> 00:01:20.670
mata ne to kimi ga te wo furu toki

00:01:20.670 --> 00:01:28.650
I feel like it has been a wonderful day

00:01:20.670 --> 00:01:28.650
ii hi datta to omoeru kara

00:01:45.510 --> 00:01:48.460
That's Big Summer Event number twelve!

00:01:48.460 --> 00:01:49.960
Hikari!

00:01:49.960 --> 00:01:51.040
Yes?

00:01:51.040 --> 00:01:53.380
Could you take these yakisoba over? {## 'bring over' is the opposite direction, usually}

00:01:53.380 --> 00:01:55.400
Roger! {## 'on my way' doesn't make sense because she's a) too close to the yakisoba to be referring to going there but also b) not yet going towards the customers}

00:01:55.400 --> 00:01:58.070
Thank you for waiting!

00:02:07.010 --> 00:02:08.570
Oh, we had the yakisoba. {## oh god what is that moustache}

00:02:08.570 --> 00:02:11.520
Nice, it's here! Looks delicious.

00:02:10.220 --> 00:02:11.520
Here you are!

00:02:11.520 --> 00:02:14.110
It feels like Big Summer Event number thirteen's coming! {## assuming she's referring to having spotted Kokoro. without 'coming' it sounds like delivering the yakisoba was number 13, which I originally parsed this as}

00:02:27.290 --> 00:02:29.670
I haven't seen that girl around before.

00:02:29.670 --> 00:02:31.730
Is she in elementary school?

00:02:33.690 --> 00:02:35.720
I must go with her!

00:02:36.350 --> 00:02:39.090
Big Summer Event confirmed!

00:03:35.410 --> 00:03:37.790
Did you see that? Did you? Octopus eggs!

00:03:37.790 --> 00:03:40.820
Yeah, yeah! The mama octopus was working hard to protect them. {there's a specific reason I didn't do this, which you could've figured out}

00:03:41.900 --> 00:03:43.200
I've never seen that before!

00:03:43.550 --> 00:03:44.950
Me neither! {## negatives with negatives}

00:03:47.740 --> 00:03:50.410
Um... who are you?

00:03:50.410 --> 00:03:53.420
Kohinata Hikari. Call me Pikari.

00:03:53.420 --> 00:03:55.070
Pikari-san?

00:03:55.070 --> 00:03:56.140
Nice to meep you! {## this what we're doing now?}

00:03:56.140 --> 00:03:57.570
Uh, sure...

00:03:58.160 --> 00:03:59.660
What's your name?

00:04:01.240 --> 00:04:02.430
Not telling.{## this is a strong voice change though, but the original is so weird coming from a kid's mouth}

00:04:09.310 --> 00:04:12.090
Oh, so you live nearby.

00:04:12.090 --> 00:04:14.890
Yes, I came here to tan.

00:04:16.670 --> 00:04:19.600
You already look a nice light brown to me.

00:04:19.600 --> 00:04:22.820
No, this isn't nearly enough! I pale in comparison to a real man of the sea! {## This/it for flow. The second part is that the original sentence means 'My tan pales in comparison to a dude.' In order for -that- to be correct we'd need 'real man of the sea's' which is gross. And also for a strengthening of the pale joke}

00:04:23.290 --> 00:04:24.780
A man... of the sea?

00:04:24.780 --> 00:04:29.950
That mama octopus has to protect her eggs all day long, right?

00:04:29.950 --> 00:04:31.420
Without even a moment of rest. {## alt. 'moment's rest'}

00:04:32.360 --> 00:04:33.930
And without eating or drinking.

00:04:35.250 --> 00:04:37.290
What a hot-blooded man. {## supported by all three of Cambridge, MW, Oxford, though if you really want to pull an exception I'll allow it}

00:04:38.480 --> 00:04:40.580
The mama octopus is female.

00:04:40.580 --> 00:04:42.970
A man among men.

00:04:43.780 --> 00:04:48.430
<i>I guess girls around her age admire that kind of manliness. {## while I understand your use of 'attract' it reads too easily as actual romantic/sexual attraction}</i>

00:04:49.400 --> 00:04:51.810
So... what's your name? {## but of course 'So' isn't a sentence by itself}

00:04:51.810 --> 00:04:53.420
Not telling. {## holy shit, i swear this was a coincidence}

00:04:56.310 --> 00:04:57.960
I'm back!

00:04:57.960 --> 00:04:59.650
Where've you been?

00:04:59.650 --> 00:05:04.780
I was off experiencing Big Summer Event 
number thirteen somewhere I didn't expect!

00:05:04.780 --> 00:05:06.110
That so?

00:05:08.880 --> 00:05:11.370
Now who could this be from...

00:05:11.950 --> 00:05:13.660
Oh, it's Teko!

00:05:13.660 --> 00:05:16.560
She's back home in the country, isn't she?

00:05:16.560 --> 00:05:18.420
Yeah, for a memorial service.

00:05:18.730 --> 00:05:20.720
<i>Pikari, what are you up to?</i>

00:05:20.720 --> 00:05:25.320
<i>Everyone else is rushing around setting up the memorial service, but I have nothing to do.</i>

00:05:25.320 --> 00:05:27.070
<i>I'll be back on the tenth.</i>

00:05:35.780 --> 00:05:40.360
<i>Let's go to a firework festival on the tenth! Together with the Diving Club... {## as adjectival, no plur. see: gun show (not guns show), film festival (films festival) etc}</i>

00:05:45.950 --> 00:05:47.680
Huh? Teko?

00:05:48.900 --> 00:05:51.080
I'd love to go to the firework festival! {## it sounds odd as a plain, because she's just accepting this here, and it becomes redundant when Pikari says it again in the next line}

00:05:51.080 --> 00:05:53.280
Yeah, let's go together!

00:05:53.280 --> 00:05:55.040
Okay, together!

00:05:55.040 --> 00:05:57.250
We should wear yukata too.

00:05:57.780 --> 00:05:58.790
Good idea!

00:05:58.790 --> 00:06:01.380
Teko, I bet you look fantastic in a yukata. {not you'll}

00:06:01.380 --> 00:06:02.550
You too!

00:06:02.550 --> 00:06:04.800
What kind of yukata do you have?

00:06:05.840 --> 00:06:07.260
That's a secret.

00:06:08.520 --> 00:06:11.710
Keep yours a secret too. It's fun to look forward to seeing it when we meet. {## I just cannot allow 'wait in expectation' to exist}

00:06:11.710 --> 00:06:13.470
<i>When we meet...</i>

00:06:14.420 --> 00:06:16.370
It's a secret to look forward to, then. {## mind the comma here if you revert, def. ', then'. I know you'd like 'secret' here, but}

00:06:16.370 --> 00:06:19.490
Oh, and I'll prepare a special ultimate weapon too!

00:06:19.490 --> 00:06:20.940
Huh? Wh-What?

00:06:20.940 --> 00:06:24.480
That one's top secret, so I extra can't tell you. {## I don't think 'top secret' can act nominally / if you must have a noun, 'super secret' or something}

00:06:25.110 --> 00:06:27.070
Top secret!

00:06:27.070 --> 00:06:28.680
Okay, got it!

00:06:28.680 --> 00:06:30.160
See you on the tenth.

00:06:30.160 --> 00:06:31.970
Okay, see you!

00:06:34.950 --> 00:06:36.630
<i>We have so many secrets.</i>

00:06:37.870 --> 00:06:41.580
<i>But... I feel really excited about it.</i>

00:06:41.950 --> 00:06:45.090
<i>In the past, whenever my friends kept secrets from me,</i>

00:06:45.090 --> 00:06:49.570
<i>I'd always assume the worst and feel anxious.</i>

00:06:51.710 --> 00:06:54.400
<i>But now I can hardly contain my anticipation.</i>

00:07:23.480 --> 00:07:25.470
It's been a week.

00:07:25.470 --> 00:07:28.460
Pikari-san! I wasn't expecting to see you here.

00:07:28.460 --> 00:07:33.470
My eyes tell me that you're having trouble finding the octopus's den because it's high tide. {## more than one rock / cf. 'because it's 7 PM' and 'due to 7 PM' / 'my eyes', my lord}

00:07:34.520 --> 00:07:38.830
You figured it out? I'm pretty sure it was around here.

00:07:39.980 --> 00:07:42.690
I'll point out where it is if you tell me your name.

00:07:46.370 --> 00:07:47.160
Ko...

00:07:47.920 --> 00:07:48.650
Ko?

00:07:50.570 --> 00:07:51.650
Kokoro...

00:07:51.650 --> 00:07:52.940
Kokoro-chan?!

00:07:52.940 --> 00:07:54.610
Don't call me that! {it's fine because he doesn't like being called Kokoro either}{## what an awful life disliking your own name}

00:07:54.610 --> 00:07:56.900
What an adorable name!

00:07:56.900 --> 00:07:59.620
This is exactly why I didn't want to say...

00:08:11.050 --> 00:08:12.550
<i>A moray! {## I feel like this exact change happens a lot}</i>

00:08:13.300 --> 00:08:14.510
<i>Kokoro-chan?</i>

00:08:57.700 --> 00:08:58.810
What's wrong?

00:08:59.680 --> 00:09:01.520
I couldn't help her. {## nothing ended up happening to the eggs, so 'protect them' is weird}

00:09:01.520 --> 00:09:04.210
Even though I was right there.

00:09:04.210 --> 00:09:07.980
The mama octopus fought so hard, but I...

00:09:12.900 --> 00:09:14.580
Kokoro-chan, look!

00:09:28.340 --> 00:09:30.750
<i>The mama octopus is okay!</i>

00:09:44.260 --> 00:09:47.470
Noon beer always tastes the best, right Kino-san? {I want to drink with her too}

00:09:48.500 --> 00:09:50.250
That's 'cause Amanchu House is the best! {## 'House's' reads immediately as the possessive here, so I actually had to read this a few times to parse correctly. Unless this is actually 'House's is'}

00:09:50.250 --> 00:09:52.230
You got that right!

00:09:52.230 --> 00:09:54.660
Mato-chan-sensei, welcome! {## 'you're here' sounds like she's been waiting for her}

00:09:54.660 --> 00:09:57.320
Oh, perfect timing. Can you get me some edamame?

00:09:57.320 --> 00:09:58.200
Sure!

00:09:58.200 --> 00:10:00.200
Roger dodger! {## while wikipedia caps it there's no reason given, and in the context of the quote it seems like it shouldn't be, since 'roger' isn't a proper either. the one book that showed up had it all decapsed, which makes sense to me}

00:10:01.160 --> 00:10:02.680
You're in high spirits.

00:10:02.680 --> 00:10:06.230
I just got back from a date with the most charming girl.

00:10:07.050 --> 00:10:10.130
This summer is staying strong in the wonderful department.

00:10:10.130 --> 00:10:15.170
Plus we're going to go see fireworks with the Diving Club! It's a mountain of wonderful.

00:10:16.940 --> 00:10:18.520
Fireworks, huh.

00:10:22.660 --> 00:10:24.630
I bet the firework festival will be fun.

00:10:24.630 --> 00:10:26.250
I'm sure it will.

00:10:26.250 --> 00:10:28.100
I can barely wait to see everyone's yuka—

00:10:28.100 --> 00:10:30.060
Ah, I forgot!

00:10:31.620 --> 00:10:35.000
I never invited Kokoro-chan to come see the fireworks... {## more explicit that she wants Kokoro with them, and not just like, 'Go see Black Panther (by yourself, I don't care), it's good'}

00:10:35.000 --> 00:10:37.950
Kokoro-chan? Oh, from your date.

00:10:37.950 --> 00:10:40.330
She'd look absolutely adorable in a yukata!

00:10:40.330 --> 00:10:43.330
But, but, but! I still have a few days until the Compass Festival!

00:10:43.330 --> 00:10:46.330
I'll see her again, right? Then I'll be able to invite her, right?! {## tense}

00:10:47.150 --> 00:10:50.150
Yeah... Also, another beer. {## -what- is going on with the punct here and I know I say this after fucking up hard yesterday}{I was probably high}

00:10:51.800 --> 00:10:54.400
A plan to fire up the fireworks even more?

00:10:54.400 --> 00:10:55.920
Yes!

00:10:57.300 --> 00:11:00.990
I'm working hard at odd jobs every day.

00:11:01.640 --> 00:11:02.720
That's so cool!

00:11:05.240 --> 00:11:06.430
Look forward to it!

00:11:07.810 --> 00:11:09.900
Hey, bring that over here.

00:11:09.900 --> 00:11:11.350
Huh? Bring what?

00:11:11.350 --> 00:11:12.130
That, right there.

00:11:12.130 --> 00:11:13.520
Oh, that!

00:11:13.870 --> 00:11:16.360
Got it. Is that all you need? {## it almost feels like there's a line missing because the pause is so clear / backgroundness aside}

00:11:17.130 --> 00:11:21.240
I don't see her. I never did get a chance to invite her.

00:11:21.950 --> 00:11:26.200
All I can do now is hope 
to find her by chance tonight.

00:11:38.460 --> 00:11:39.800
Pikari!

00:11:40.650 --> 00:11:41.760
Teko!

00:11:42.710 --> 00:11:45.010
A goldfish yukata!

00:11:46.770 --> 00:11:50.080
And yours has sunflowers. It's just like you!

00:11:50.080 --> 00:11:51.690
You look great in yours too! {for the sake of variation (and yuri), the niatteru applies to both her and her outfit}

00:11:53.290 --> 00:11:54.460
Hey.

00:11:56.140 --> 00:11:58.740
Mato-chan-sensei, where's your yukata?!

00:11:58.740 --> 00:12:03.760
I don't need one. You youngins just need to look good enough for me too.

00:12:05.420 --> 00:12:06.720
We're here.

00:12:09.020 --> 00:12:11.410
Ane-chan-senpai skipped the yukata too...

00:12:11.410 --> 00:12:13.790
So the twins came in jinbei.

00:12:14.390 --> 00:12:17.560
I even told everyone to wear yukata...

00:12:17.560 --> 00:12:19.210
Pikari...

00:12:19.210 --> 00:12:21.960
These are much more comfortable.

00:12:21.960 --> 00:12:24.530
Are you sure you didn't want to wear one? {## 'it' has an unclear referent / this needs to be a generic one (cf. something like 'yours', which I initially had) because there's a specific one mentioned later that, so there have to be multiple/generic}

00:12:24.530 --> 00:12:29.490
You even had your old monkey-pattern yukata repaired.

00:12:29.490 --> 00:12:30.600
Monkey?

00:12:30.600 --> 00:12:34.020
She threw a tantrum over wanting to use that pattern,

00:12:34.020 --> 00:12:35.230
so she had it made—

00:12:34.020 --> 00:12:35.230
Cheerio! {## hey! you can't just do this}{ai-chan doya}

00:12:36.620 --> 00:12:37.790
Monkey.

00:12:37.790 --> 00:12:39.380
So cute.

00:12:39.380 --> 00:12:42.280
I'd like to see it at least once.

00:12:42.930 --> 00:12:45.140
That was a folly of youth!

00:12:45.140 --> 00:12:46.660
We don't mind if you want to change.

00:12:46.660 --> 00:12:48.370
Well <i>I</i> do!

00:12:48.370 --> 00:12:50.360
Come on, Makoto. Get up already!

00:12:50.360 --> 00:12:53.110
We wanna see!

00:12:51.610 --> 00:12:53.110
Shut up!

00:12:53.110 --> 00:12:55.080
Let's get a move on already!

00:13:05.710 --> 00:13:06.800
Pikari?

00:13:06.800 --> 00:13:08.670
Are you looking for someone?

00:13:08.670 --> 00:13:10.550
Yeah, Kokoro-chan.

00:13:10.550 --> 00:13:11.890
Kokoro-chan?

00:13:11.890 --> 00:13:15.270
Oh, that girl you said you had a date with?

00:13:15.950 --> 00:13:17.240
You had a date? {## something about 'A date?' -> 'With ...' seems off}

00:13:17.240 --> 00:13:19.900
With this super cute girl I met at the sea!

00:13:22.440 --> 00:13:26.160
She said she was from the area, so I was hoping she'd be here.

00:13:27.100 --> 00:13:28.620
You didn't make plans?

00:13:28.620 --> 00:13:31.130
I really wanted to, but... {## she really didn't try, though}

00:13:31.130 --> 00:13:33.920
So you have no idea whether you'll see her tonight?

00:13:33.920 --> 00:13:36.080
Not an inkling...

00:13:37.410 --> 00:13:39.790
<i>She wanted to make plans... {## also just 'meet her' sounds like she never has / if you want, 'meet up with'}</i>

00:13:41.050 --> 00:13:43.230
<i>That's completely normal!</i>

00:13:43.230 --> 00:13:48.390
<i>Pikari gets along with everyone, so this is a completely normal, expected thing!</i>

00:13:49.390 --> 00:13:52.590
I hope you run into Kokoro-chan. {## alt. 'end up running'}

00:13:53.710 --> 00:13:54.600
Thanks.

00:13:55.950 --> 00:13:58.490
But man, everywhere we go is packed.

00:13:58.490 --> 00:14:00.800
Makoto, do something about this.

00:14:00.800 --> 00:14:02.070
I can't.

00:14:04.850 --> 00:14:11.280
The time is ripe to reveal the full extent of my fire-up-the-fireworks plan. {## '"Fire Up the Fireworks Plan"'}

00:14:11.280 --> 00:14:11.990
What's that?

00:14:11.990 --> 00:14:16.330
I have an ultimate weapon prepared!

00:14:20.630 --> 00:14:21.960
You didn't...

00:14:21.960 --> 00:14:23.300
Here?

00:14:23.300 --> 00:14:25.250
These are our seats? {here here is for knights}

00:14:25.250 --> 00:14:27.010
That's right!

00:14:27.530 --> 00:14:31.030
How'd you get us these VIP-tier seats? {## 'get this ... place' sounds like, I dunno, it's weird. first off, like she got the whole place, second, ugh. it'd be VIP-Only-like too, I think, though I'm not 100% and really it's an indication that you shouldn't be doing that}

00:14:32.030 --> 00:14:34.070
And we're in the front row too.

00:14:36.120 --> 00:14:39.400
I worked hard every day just for tonight!

00:14:39.400 --> 00:14:40.760
Pikari!

00:14:40.760 --> 00:14:42.470
Amazing!

00:14:49.160 --> 00:14:53.540
This is the top secret you were 
talking about? What a surprise!

00:14:53.540 --> 00:14:56.030
Mission success!

00:14:56.030 --> 00:14:58.360
I guess I won't be able to call you Bakari today.

00:14:58.360 --> 00:14:59.540
Just today?!

00:15:00.200 --> 00:15:04.330
Pikari's secrets are always fun surprises. {## secrets don't hide things, secrets are things that -are hidden-}

00:15:06.050 --> 00:15:08.280
You're quite the secret-lover yourself, Teko!

00:15:08.280 --> 00:15:10.260
Yeah, I am!

00:15:10.260 --> 00:15:13.480
And you know, I only learned that today.

00:15:14.320 --> 00:15:17.090
That's Big Summer Event number fourteen!

00:15:17.090 --> 00:15:19.040
What? Fourteen?

00:15:19.040 --> 00:15:20.350
Thank you for your patience.

00:15:20.740 --> 00:15:24.600
I'm proud to present the 
Compass Festival's beach fireworks.

00:15:26.820 --> 00:15:28.240
It started!

00:15:35.640 --> 00:15:38.410
Tamaya! {no fucks now, maybe later}{## don't forget to give fucks}{nope, fresh out}

00:15:44.830 --> 00:15:46.840
They're huge!

00:15:47.590 --> 00:15:52.320
<i>I've never seen fireworks from this angle or this close up! {## 'straight up' is just}</i>

00:15:53.800 --> 00:15:55.160
This is incredible.

00:15:55.160 --> 00:15:57.510
I can feel the sound shaking in my stomach. {## but mostly not 'resonating', orig. makes it sound like his stomach is a bagpipe or something}

00:15:58.390 --> 00:16:01.980
<i>This sight... These fireworks...</i>

00:16:03.650 --> 00:16:07.560
<i>After I tried my best to get through the crowd in an uncomfortable yukata, {## just 'doing' is an extended timeframe, which probably wants a stative / 'weave' is what you really want, but formal-er / also I'm pretty sure this is a dangling participle}</i>

00:16:07.560 --> 00:16:12.670
<i>the fireworks I finally saw through the gaps in the people seemed far-off and pitiful. {## 'were' really denotes that they actually -were-, which isn't the case as seen later / 'gaps of people' is NG. it'd be 'gaps between people' but still not good}{no crush}</i>

00:16:15.610 --> 00:16:20.080
<i>It was then that his large hand reached out to me.</i>

00:16:21.170 --> 00:16:25.790
<i>Saying it would be easier for me to find my parents, the view I saw while riding on his shoulders</i>

00:16:27.050 --> 00:16:32.520
<i>was like a flower field of fireworks stretching all 360 degrees across the sky. {## 'in 360 degrees' sounds like it's in 360 different intensities or something}</i>

00:16:33.160 --> 00:16:36.850
<i>It was so magnificent I thought it had to be a dream...</i>

00:16:37.590 --> 00:16:40.300
<i>But these are the very same fireworks.</i>

00:16:40.300 --> 00:16:42.310
<i>It wasn't a dream.</i>

00:16:43.900 --> 00:16:46.780
<i>I wonder when I convinced myself it had to be.</i>

00:16:50.270 --> 00:16:51.990
<i>I have Pikari to thank.</i>

00:16:52.370 --> 00:16:57.050
<i>What a wonderful secret. This is what Pikari wanted to share!</i>

00:16:58.250 --> 00:17:01.700
<i>She must've worked so hard for this.</i>

00:17:01.700 --> 00:17:04.220
Teko, wasn't that one pretty?

00:17:04.220 --> 00:17:05.330
Yeah.

00:17:06.620 --> 00:17:11.160
<i>It's no wonder that everyone comes to love Pikari.</i>

00:17:11.960 --> 00:17:15.720
This'll definitely be the most memorable event of the summer.

00:17:15.720 --> 00:17:17.680
Forget this summer— {## initial thought 'Not just for this summer'  / 'of my whole life!' for maximum parallel but it didn't work out / original just doesn't work out properly}

00:17:18.000 --> 00:17:20.180
I'll remember this for the rest of my life!

00:17:20.180 --> 00:17:21.280
Me too!

00:17:23.660 --> 00:17:25.120
I'm so touched!

00:17:25.120 --> 00:17:27.520
Thank you, Pikari!

00:17:27.520 --> 00:17:30.110
Please, please, please!

00:17:35.140 --> 00:17:38.410
I hope Kokoro-chan is watching the fireworks too.

00:17:38.950 --> 00:17:39.800
Yeah!

00:17:52.130 --> 00:17:53.380
There she is.

00:17:55.500 --> 00:17:57.230
Good morning, Kokoro-chan.

00:17:57.760 --> 00:18:03.100
I looked for you everywhere during yesterday's firework festival, but didn't end up finding you.

00:18:04.450 --> 00:18:05.480
Kokoro-chan?

00:18:06.430 --> 00:18:09.710
The mama octopus has been missing... since yesterday.

00:18:10.970 --> 00:18:12.710
She hasn't come back.

00:18:32.430 --> 00:18:33.590
<i>Kokoro-chan!</i>

00:18:37.310 --> 00:18:38.130
Where are you going?

00:18:38.130 --> 00:18:40.770
Further out to look for the mama octopus! {## the sea is an exterior direction. if for some reason you meant deeper, then 'down'}

00:18:40.770 --> 00:18:41.910
You can't, it's too dangerous!

00:18:41.910 --> 00:18:43.260
Please let me go!

00:18:43.260 --> 00:18:44.470
Kokoro-chan!

00:18:45.530 --> 00:18:47.770
I have to save the mama octopus!

00:18:47.770 --> 00:18:48.730
Calm down!

00:18:48.730 --> 00:18:49.390
Let go!

00:18:49.390 --> 00:18:49.850
No!

00:18:50.270 --> 00:18:51.610
Get off!

00:18:57.030 --> 00:18:59.910
I thought... she'd be okay.

00:19:00.840 --> 00:19:05.550
Even after she fought off the moray and got beat up, she still came back. {## 'even ... still' gross}

00:19:06.420 --> 00:19:09.090
So I thought... she'd make it.

00:19:09.860 --> 00:19:11.380
Her eggs were safe.  {## 'were' implies that they either aren't safe anymore, or hatched safely and thus aren't eggs anymore, neither of which is the case. It also doesn't lead properly into the next sentences}{no, /this/ sounds like it's past tense only. were means just now when pikari checked}

00:19:17.170 --> 00:19:24.750
Octopus mothers spend an entire month fasting to defend their unhatched eggs. {## subject fuckery / protect protect because of above change}

00:19:25.610 --> 00:19:28.010
They eventually starve to death.

00:19:28.010 --> 00:19:31.830
One way or another, her fate was already sealed. {## 'No matter how it happened' reads as though 'no matter how [some event] happened, [that event] sealed her fate'. 'whether A or B, same fate'. What you actually want is for the fates to be -different- (the difference in the manner of death, here) but both -sealed- (in -being- death)}

00:19:34.850 --> 00:19:36.940
Without the mama octopus...

00:19:36.940 --> 00:19:39.890
what'll happen to the eggs? {## one sentence}

00:19:41.040 --> 00:19:46.120
If they're lucky, they'll avoid being spotted by another animal and survive.

00:19:47.350 --> 00:19:52.190
If they're not, a predator might eat them before they hatch. {## orig. 'before the eggs are born', but of course eggs aren't born. no way to switch subject to interior octolets, so. prev. unhatched is far enough away}

00:19:53.690 --> 00:19:57.320
That's how the circle of life turns in the ocean.

00:20:38.100 --> 00:20:43.330
<i>The ocean brings us miraculous encounters.</i>

00:20:43.950 --> 00:20:46.760
<i>While it may have just been a coincidence, {## lots of things here. Firstly 'it' refers to [the ocean bringing them miraculous encounters], not to that one specifically.  'Some may' puts the focus on the some, and the expectation is a contrast with 'but -I- call it fate' or something like that, so it's odd when it doesn't (what's -actually- being contrasted is coincidence v. meaning).  'might' and 'may' further have shades of nuance to them in that context (I think 'may' implies other people know, and 'might' implies they don't but would if they did, but I don't have evidence).}</i>

00:20:47.370 --> 00:20:54.230
<i>for the two of us, it shined a light down upon our day and our summer. {## note for tense change: there's just absolutely no reason to use the pres. perfect. it sounds weird, means something weird, just no reason}</i>

00:21:04.620 --> 00:21:12.610
<i>Being in the sea like this... there are times when I believe that a god may really exist.</i>

00:21:14.550 --> 00:21:19.090
<i>Look, even though we weren't with each other to see the whole sky covered in fireworks, {## Hikari definitely (and Kokoro possibly) -did- see that sky, this is specifically about 'together / not together' not 'seeing /not seeing'}</i>

00:21:19.090 --> 00:21:24.300
<i>we were together to see the moment the whole sea was filled with tiny lives.</i>

00:21:28.750 --> 00:21:31.340
<i>So, Kokoro-chan, {## 'That's why ... let's' is just wrong}</i>

00:21:34.790 --> 00:21:39.890
<i>when you're able to look back on this wonderful summer memory with a smile,</i>

00:21:40.830 --> 00:21:42.910
<i>let's go to Amanchu House together!</i>

00:21:45.860 --> 00:21:49.600
<i>We'll stuff our mouths with pork soup like a couple of manly men!</i>

